
I built a Windows Active Directory environment from scratch in my home lab to understand how Windows domains work and to get practical experience with domain administration, users, groups, DNS, Group Policy and Windows security.

I also wanted to understand how Active Directory activity can be useful from a SOC Analyst point of view, especially when investigating authentication and security events.

This project was built using virtual machines in VirtualBox.

Project Goals

The main goals of this lab were to:
	•	Build an Active Directory domain from scratch.
	•	Configure a Windows Server as a Domain Controller.
	•	Understand the role of DNS in Active Directory.
	•	Create and manage domain users and groups.
	•	Create Organizational Units (OUs).
	•	Join a Windows client computer to the domain.
	•	Understand and apply Group Policy.
	•	Explore Windows security and authentication events.
	•	Understand how Active Directory can generate useful security information for a SOC Analyst.

Lab Environment

Virtualization
	•	VirtualBox

Machines

Windows Server
	•	Used as the Domain Controller.
	•	Hosted Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS).
	•	Provided domain authentication and directory services.
	•	Also handled DNS for the Active Directory environment.

Windows 10 Client
	•	Used as the domain-joined workstation.
	•	Used to test user authentication and domain policies.

Active Directory Domain

I created my own test domain for the lab:

[CMDDC.local]

The Windows Server was promoted to a Domain Controller and became the central server responsible for managing the domain.

1. Installing Active Directory Domain Services

I started by installing the Active Directory Domain Services role on Windows Server.

After the role was installed, I used the Server Manager notification to promote the server to a Domain Controller.

During the setup, I created the new Active Directory forest and configured the domain used for the lab.

After the server restarted, I was able to log in using the domain administrator account.

What I learned

I learned that installing the AD DS role is not the same thing as creating the Active Directory environment.

The server first needs the AD DS role, and then it has to be promoted to a Domain Controller so that it can actually provide Active Directory services.

2. Understanding the Domain Controller

The Domain Controller became the central part of my lab.

It is responsible for authenticating domain users and providing access to Active Directory services.

This helped me understand why the Domain Controller is such an important system from a security perspective.

If an attacker gains control of a Domain Controller, the impact can be much larger than compromising a normal workstation because the Domain Controller controls important identity and authentication functions across the domain.

3. Active Directory Users and Groups

I created test users inside Active Directory to simulate employees in a small organization.

I also created groups so that users could be managed according to their role.

Example structure:

Active Directory
│
├── IT
├── SOC
├── HR
└── Users

Example test groups:

IT-Admins
SOC-Analysts
HR-Users

Users could then be added to the appropriate groups.

What I learned

One important thing I learned was the difference between users and groups.

Instead of giving permissions to every user individually, groups make it easier to manage users according to their role.

This becomes especially important when an organization has many users.

4. Organizational Units

I created Organizational Units to organize users and computers.

For example:

CyberLab
│
├── IT
├── SOC
├── HR
└── Workstations

The purpose of the Organization Unit was not just organization.

I also learned that OUs are useful because Group Policies can be applied to specific OUs.

This means different departments or computers can receive different policies depending on how the Active Directory environment is organized.

5. Joining Windows 10 to the Domain

After setting up Active Directory, I connected the Windows 10 virtual machine to the domain.

The Windows 10 machine was successfully joined to the Active Directory domain.

I then tested logging in using a domain account.

Example:

AWHYS

This confirmed that the client was communicating with the Domain Controller and that the Active Directory authentication process was working.

