6. Group Policy

I also explored Group Policy to understand how an administrator can centrally control Windows computers in a domain.

For this lab, I focused on security-related settings such as:
	•	Password policies
	•	Account lockout settings
	•	Windows security settings
	•	Firewall-related settings
	•	Audit policies

The main thing I learned from this was that Group Policy allows administrators to manage security settings centrally instead of configuring every computer manually.

7. Active Directory and Security Monitoring

One of the main reasons I wanted to build this lab was to understand Active Directory from a SOC Analyst perspective.

User authentication generates security events in Windows.

For example:

Event ID 4624

represents a successful logon.

Another important event is:

Event ID 4625

which represents a failed logon attempt.

These events can provide useful information such as:
	•	Which account was involved.
	•	When the activity happened.
	•	Where the authentication attempt came from.
	•	Whether the login succeeded or failed.

This helped me understand how a SOC Analyst could use Windows event logs to investigate suspicious authentication activity.

8. Authentication Investigation

I tested authentication activity inside my lab and examined the resulting Windows Security events.

For failed authentication attempts, I investigated Event ID 4625.

The investigation questions I focused on were:

Who was the targeted user?

When did the attempt happen?

Was the authentication successful or unsuccessful?

Which computer generated the activity?

Does the activity look normal or suspicious?

This helped me connect the Active Directory lab with the security monitoring concepts I have been learning.

9. Security Perspective

Building the Active Directory environment also helped me understand why identity management is important in cybersecurity.

A SOC Analyst may investigate activity such as:
	•	Multiple failed login attempts.
	•	Suspicious successful logins.
	•	Account lockouts.
	•	Unexpected use of privileged accounts.
	•	Authentication from unusual systems.

Active Directory therefore provides an important source of security telemetry that can be investigated during an incident.

10. Challenges I Encountered

This lab was not completely straightforward.

I had some problems while building and configuring the virtual machines, including issues with the Windows Server environment and VirtualBox resources.

Working through these problems helped me become more comfortable with troubleshooting virtual machines, networking and Windows Server configuration.

One of the most useful lessons for me was that troubleshooting is part of building a real lab. Getting the final result working was important, but understanding why something was not working was also part of the learning process.

